S3 paired baseline — what an integration can see of the home

  the home: 36 entities, of which 20 disclose occupancy, presence or routine
  declared: sensor.outdoor_temperature

                                        unconfined    confined
  entities present to it                    36/36        1/36 
  of those, occupancy or presence           20/20        0/20 
  changes by others delivered to it        4/4   none, see below

  probe                                   unconfined  confined
  sensor.outdoor_temperature              read        present; read + subscribe permitted
  device_tracker.younes_phone *           read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read
  binary_sensor.bedroom_occupancy *       read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read
  lock.front_door *                       read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read
  alarm_control_panel.home_alarm *        read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read
  calendar.family *                       read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read
  sensor.electricity_price                read        absent; denied /permissions/entities/read

  * discloses occupancy, presence or routine

  On the delivery of other integrations' changes: unconfined, all 4 of 4 arrive
  through EVENT_STATE_CHANGED, unasked and unfiltered. Confined, none arrive — but
  **that is an absent feature, not a demonstrated filter**: §6.11.2 records that no
  push loop exists, so declared entities are mirrored once at construction and there
  is no path by which any state change could arrive. S3's subscription half is
  therefore verified at the decision layer only, and §7.14.4 says so.
PASS — S3, both halves.
       Unconfined, the integration holds all 36 entities including every one of the
       20 that disclose presence, and is told about every change to entities it
       does not own.
       Confined, 6 of 6 undeclared probes are not in its state machine at all,
       every mediated read and subscription is refused with a clause, and the one
       entity it declared is readable.
       The subscription half is a decision, not a delivered stream — see the note above.
